==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|trousse|lang=fro}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|/tɹʌs/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|trusses}}

# A [[bandage]] and [[belt]] used to hold a [[hernia]] in place.
#* {{quote-book
|year=2008
|author=Lippincott Williams & Wilkins
|title=Professional Guide to Diseases
|chapter=4
|isbn=0781778999
|page=280
|passage=A '''truss''' may keep the abdominal contents from protruding into the hernial sac; however, this won't cure the hernia.}}
# {{context|architecture|lang=en}} A [[structure]] made up of one or more [[triangular]] units made from straight [[beam]]s of wood or metal, which is used to support a structure as in a [[roof]] or [[bridge]].
# {{context|architecture|lang=en}} A [[triangular]] [[bracket]].
# An old English farming [[measurement]]. One truss of straw equalled 36 pounds, a truss of old hay equalled 56 pounds, a truss of new hay equalled 60 pounds, and 36 trusses equalled one load.
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} A bundle; a package.
#* Spenser
#*: bearing a '''truss''' of trifles at his back
# {{context|historical|lang=en}} A padded jacket or dress worn under armour, to protect the body from the effects of [[friction]].
#* Drayton
#*: Puts off his palmer's weed unto his '''truss''', which bore / The stains of ancient arms.
# {{context|historical|lang=en}} Part of a woman's dress; a [[stomacher]].
# {{context|botany|lang=en}} A [[tuft]] of [[flower]]s formed at the top of the main [[stem]] of certain plants.
# {{context|nautical|lang=en}} The rope or iron used to keep the centre of a [[yard]] to the [[mast]].

